1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,525
Why Singapore's Merchants
Asked to Leave British India:

2
00:00:04,605 --> 00:00:07,200
The 1867 Transfer to Crown Rule

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:11,210
At noon on Monday, the 1st
of April 1867, a salute of

4
00:00:11,290 --> 00:00:15,916
guns from Fort Canning announced
a ceremony at the Town Hall,

5
00:00:15,996 --> 00:00:19,700
the building that is today
the Victoria Theatre.

6
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:23,884
A crowd gathered outside, and
inside, the Order constituting

7
00:00:23,964 --> 00:00:28,300
the Straits Settlements a colony
of the Crown was read aloud.

8
00:00:28,300 --> 00:00:32,933
For four decades, Singapore, Penang
and Malacca had been governed

9
00:00:33,012 --> 00:00:37,725
as an outlying part of British
India, under orders from Calcutta.

10
00:00:37,725 --> 00:00:40,332
From that day they answered to the

11
00:00:40,412 --> 00:00:43,100
Colonial Office in London instead.

12
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:46,725
The change had not been
imposed from above.

13
00:00:46,725 --> 00:00:52,087
It was the result of a campaign of more
than ten years by Singapore's own merchants,

14
00:00:52,167 --> 00:00:55,716
who had grown tired of being
ruled by a government that,

15
00:00:55,796 --> 00:00:59,100
in their view, understood
little about their trade.

16
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:01,925
According to the historian C. B. Buckley,

17
00:01:02,005 --> 00:01:07,947
who described the ceremony in his Anecdotal
History of Old Times in Singapore (1902),

18
00:01:08,027 --> 00:01:10,711
the acting governor, Colonel Henry Man,

19
00:01:10,791 --> 00:01:14,900
arrived first and went round
the room greeting the guests.

20
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:17,637
Then the new governor, Colonel Harry Ord,

21
00:01:17,717 --> 00:01:21,485
walked in under another salute
without removing his hat,

22
00:01:21,565 --> 00:01:25,275
and sat down on the dais
without acknowledging anyone.

23
00:01:25,275 --> 00:01:29,825
Buckley wrote that the impression
this created "was never removed".

24
00:01:29,825 --> 00:01:33,500
Admiral Sir Henry Keppel, a
popular figure in Singapore,

25
00:01:33,580 --> 00:01:39,065
arrived next, shook hands with the people
he knew, and declined a seat on the dais,

26
00:01:39,145 --> 00:01:42,900
taking an ordinary Town Hall
chair on the floor instead.

27
00:01:42,900 --> 00:01:46,750
The documents were read
and the oaths administered.

28
00:01:46,750 --> 00:01:51,675
Then the members of the new
Legislative Council were sworn in.

29
00:01:51,675 --> 00:01:55,230
Among them was William Henry
Read, a merchant who had

30
00:01:55,310 --> 00:01:59,425
chaired many of the public
meetings that led to that moment.

31
00:01:59,425 --> 00:02:02,992
For the first time, men
from outside the government

32
00:02:03,072 --> 00:02:06,575
sat on a council that made
the colony's own laws.

33
00:02:06,575 --> 00:02:11,055
Penang, Malacca and Singapore
were joined together as the

34
00:02:11,135 --> 00:02:15,775
Straits Settlements in 1826,
under the East India Company.

35
00:02:15,775 --> 00:02:19,456
In 1830 they were reduced
to a residency under

36
00:02:19,536 --> 00:02:23,790
the Bengal Presidency, and
from 1851 they were placed

37
00:02:23,870 --> 00:02:27,550
directly under the
Governor-General of India.

38
00:02:27,550 --> 00:02:32,107
Laws for Singapore were made in
Calcutta, and its affairs were

39
00:02:32,187 --> 00:02:36,525
one small item among the many
problems of governing India.

40
00:02:36,525 --> 00:02:39,600
Yet the settlements were growing fast.

41
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:45,033
When Singapore's petition for
a transfer reached the House of

42
00:02:45,113 --> 00:02:50,276
Commons in April 1858, Lord
Bury told the House that their

43
00:02:50,356 --> 00:02:56,050
trade had risen from £4 million
in 1840 to £15 million in 1857.

44
00:02:56,050 --> 00:02:59,967
Singapore's merchants felt that
their interests lay with the

45
00:03:00,047 --> 00:03:03,632
trade passing between Europe
and China, not with India,

46
00:03:03,712 --> 00:03:08,575
and that Calcutta treated the Straits
mainly as a place to send convicts.

47
00:03:08,575 --> 00:03:12,050
The first quarrel was about money itself.

48
00:03:12,050 --> 00:03:15,562
Trade in South-East Asia
had long been conducted in

49
00:03:15,642 --> 00:03:19,647
Spanish and Mexican silver
dollars, as told in the post on

50
00:03:19,727 --> 00:03:23,310
the Spanish dollar, and the
Malay, Chinese and Bugis

51
00:03:23,390 --> 00:03:27,475
traders who came to Singapore
expected to be paid in them.

52
00:03:27,475 --> 00:03:33,134
The Government of India wanted one
currency across its territories,

53
00:03:33,214 --> 00:03:37,074
and in currency laws of
1835, 1847 and 1855 it

54
00:03:37,154 --> 00:03:41,950
tried to make the Indian rupee
the money of the Straits.

55
00:03:41,950 --> 00:03:47,467
For a time, the treasury in Singapore
was told to make its payments

56
00:03:47,547 --> 00:03:52,475
in rupees instead of dollars,
at 220 rupees to 100 dollars.

57
00:03:52,475 --> 00:03:56,552
The merchants feared that traders
from the region would not accept

58
00:03:56,632 --> 00:04:00,600
the new coins, and that the change
would damage their business.

59
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:03,963
They held public meetings,
protested against what

60
00:04:04,043 --> 00:04:07,406
one resolution called "the
insidious introduction

61
00:04:07,486 --> 00:04:11,000
of the Rupee Currency",
and petitioned Parliament.

62
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:15,824
In the House of Lords in April 1856,
the Earl of Albemarle presented

63
00:04:15,904 --> 00:04:20,224
their petition and described
the rupee's small change as "the

64
00:04:20,304 --> 00:04:24,775
most barbarous and inconvenient
coin that could be conceived".

65
00:04:24,775 --> 00:04:29,098
The bill to make Indian copper
coins legal tender in the Straits

66
00:04:29,178 --> 00:04:33,375
did not pass, and the dollar
remained the money of Singapore.

67
00:04:33,375 --> 00:04:36,750
The second quarrel was
about the free port.

68
00:04:36,750 --> 00:04:40,570
Since its founding in 1819,
Singapore had charged no

69
00:04:40,650 --> 00:04:44,470
duties on the ships and
goods that used its harbour,

70
00:04:44,550 --> 00:04:49,050
and the merchants regarded this
as the basis of its success.

71
00:04:49,050 --> 00:04:52,821
In 1856 the Government
of India proposed to

72
00:04:52,901 --> 00:04:57,200
levy tonnage dues on ships
arriving at the port.

73
00:04:57,200 --> 00:05:02,228
According to The Straits Times
of the 23rd of December 1856,

74
00:05:02,308 --> 00:05:07,932
the aim was to cover the cost of
the Master Attendant's department,

75
00:05:08,012 --> 00:05:10,225
about 8,000 rupees a year.

76
00:05:10,225 --> 00:05:13,900
The sum was small, but
the principle was not.

77
00:05:13,900 --> 00:05:18,022
At a crowded public meeting
on the 18th of December 1856,

78
00:05:18,102 --> 00:05:21,856
with W. H. Read in the
chair, the merchants resolved

79
00:05:21,936 --> 00:05:25,690
that the dues were "an
unwarrantable attack upon the

80
00:05:25,770 --> 00:05:29,825
freedom of this port", and
sent a memorial to Calcutta.

81
00:05:29,825 --> 00:05:32,858
In 1857 the Company's directors

82
00:05:32,938 --> 00:05:35,650
in London forbade the dues.

83
00:05:35,650 --> 00:05:40,100
The proposal returned in 1863,
when the Governor of the Straits,

84
00:05:40,180 --> 00:05:42,860
Colonel Orfeur Cavenagh, sided with the

85
00:05:42,940 --> 00:05:46,975
Chamber of Commerce against
it, and it was dropped again.

86
00:05:46,975 --> 00:05:51,825
The third set of complaints was
about how Calcutta used the Straits.

87
00:05:51,825 --> 00:05:56,838
Convicts from across India had been
shipped to Singapore since 1825,

88
00:05:56,918 --> 00:06:02,231
as told in the post on the Indian
convicts who built colonial Singapore,

89
00:06:02,311 --> 00:06:05,751
and in 1858 Lord Bury
told the Commons that the

90
00:06:05,831 --> 00:06:10,625
convicts of Bombay, Madras and
Bengal were all being sent there.

91
00:06:10,625 --> 00:06:15,613
In 1857, the uprising in India made
the merchants more uneasy about

92
00:06:15,693 --> 00:06:20,379
the convict population, and it
also brought public works in the

93
00:06:20,459 --> 00:06:25,225
Straits to a halt, as India cut
spending to pay for the crisis.

94
00:06:25,225 --> 00:06:28,420
In 1860, Calcutta proposed to extend

95
00:06:28,500 --> 00:06:31,775
an Indian income tax to the Straits.

96
00:06:31,775 --> 00:06:34,624
A public meeting called it unsuited to the

97
00:06:34,704 --> 00:06:38,949
settlements and "framed in total
ignorance" of their finances,

98
00:06:39,029 --> 00:06:41,400
and the proposal was soon dropped.

99
00:06:41,400 --> 00:06:45,875
The idea of leaving Indian
rule was first raised at a

100
00:06:45,955 --> 00:06:51,025
public meeting in Singapore in
August 1855, and voted down.

101
00:06:51,025 --> 00:06:55,440
Two years later, in September
1857, a meeting of the European

102
00:06:55,520 --> 00:07:00,008
merchants resolved to petition
Parliament, and in October most

103
00:07:00,088 --> 00:07:04,577
of Singapore's European community
signed a petition asking for

104
00:07:04,657 --> 00:07:09,225
the Straits Settlements to be
placed directly under the Crown.

105
00:07:09,225 --> 00:07:11,900
Lord Bury presented it in the

106
00:07:11,980 --> 00:07:14,925
House of Commons in April 1858.

107
00:07:14,925 --> 00:07:19,675
Even India's Governor-General,
Lord Canning, came to agree.

108
00:07:19,675 --> 00:07:23,292
In a minute written in November
1859, he observed that the

109
00:07:23,372 --> 00:07:26,989
Chinese of the Straits were
very different from the people

110
00:07:27,069 --> 00:07:30,749
Indian officials were trained
to govern, and concluded that

111
00:07:30,829 --> 00:07:34,255
unless officers were specially
trained for the Straits,

112
00:07:34,335 --> 00:07:38,350
"the Indian Government cannot do
justice to these Settlements".

113
00:07:38,350 --> 00:07:42,425
The campaign continued into the 1860s.

114
00:07:42,425 --> 00:07:47,004
At a Town Hall meeting in December
1863 that prepared a further

115
00:07:47,084 --> 00:07:52,255
report in favour of the transfer,
Seah Eu Chin was recorded as present,

116
00:07:52,335 --> 00:07:56,550
the only Chinese merchant
named among those who attended.

117
00:07:56,550 --> 00:08:00,740
London accepted the transfer in
principle, but it was held up for

118
00:08:00,820 --> 00:08:04,944
years by questions of money, in
particular who would pay for the

119
00:08:05,024 --> 00:08:09,425
troops and the fortifications India
had been building at Singapore.

120
00:08:09,425 --> 00:08:14,488
The Act of Parliament to provide
for the government of the Straits

121
00:08:14,568 --> 00:08:19,008
Settlements was finally passed
on the 10th of August 1866,

122
00:08:19,088 --> 00:08:24,075
and an Order in Council fixed the
1st of April 1867 as the date.

123
00:08:24,075 --> 00:08:28,384
The new Crown colony had its own
governor, an Executive Council

124
00:08:28,464 --> 00:08:32,355
and a Legislative Council,
with a few unofficial members,

125
00:08:32,435 --> 00:08:36,475
drawn from the merchants,
sitting alongside the officials.

126
00:08:36,475 --> 00:08:38,944
Hoo Ah Kay, better known as Whampoa,

127
00:08:39,024 --> 00:08:43,625
later joined the Legislative
Council as its first Chinese member.

128
00:08:43,625 --> 00:08:47,968
The settlements were now governed
as a colony in their own right,

129
00:08:48,048 --> 00:08:51,450
from London, rather than
as the far edge of India.

130
00:08:51,450 --> 00:08:55,200
The merchants did not get
everything they hoped for.

131
00:08:55,200 --> 00:08:59,431
Governor Ord had his own views
and a forceful manner, and he was

132
00:08:59,511 --> 00:09:04,025
soon at odds with the community
that had campaigned for the change.

133
00:09:04,025 --> 00:09:08,100
But the two things they had
fought hardest for survived.

134
00:09:08,100 --> 00:09:11,020
The dollar remained Singapore's currency,

135
00:09:11,100 --> 00:09:13,075
and the port remained free.

136
00:09:13,075 --> 00:09:16,325
Both survive in some form today.

137
00:09:16,325 --> 00:09:19,717
Singapore still has its
own dollar, and it still

138
00:09:19,797 --> 00:09:23,262
charges customs duty on
only four kinds of goods,

139
00:09:23,342 --> 00:09:27,096
according to Singapore
Customs: intoxicating liquors,

140
00:09:27,176 --> 00:09:31,300
tobacco products, motor
vehicles, and petroleum products.

141
00:09:31,300 --> 00:09:36,082
Almost everything else enters
without duty, although the goods and

142
00:09:36,162 --> 00:09:40,950
services tax applies to imports
as it does to goods sold at home.

143
00:09:40,950 --> 00:09:44,335
The quarrels of the
1850s were, in a sense,

144
00:09:44,415 --> 00:09:47,800
an early argument over the same principle.

145
00:09:47,800 --> 00:09:52,178
Why it matters today: The transfer
of 1867 is usually remembered,

146
00:09:52,258 --> 00:09:56,842
if at all, as a change of office
in London, but it was won by people

147
00:09:56,922 --> 00:10:02,466
in Singapore arguing for their own interests,
and two of the things they defended,

148
00:10:02,546 --> 00:10:06,250
the dollar and the free port,
still shape the country.
